<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Frederick I, Elector of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1503">1503</a>–<a href="/years/1554">1554</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as John Frederick the Magnanimous · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Frederick I, called the Magnanimous, was Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> from 1532 to 1547 and a leader of the Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>. Defeated and captured by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a> in 1547, he lost the electoral title and much of his land to his cousin Maurice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-line">Albertine line</a>. Released in 1552, he had planned the school at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, opened in 1548, that became its university.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-frederick-i-elector-of-saxony.jpg" alt="John Frederick I, Elector of Saxony" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_d._%C3%84._044FXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Frederick I was born on 30 June 1503, the eldest son of John, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, by his first wife Sophie of Mecklenburg-Schwerin, who died fourteen days later. George Spalatin, a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, educated him, and he became devoted to Luther's teaching. His library was one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He urged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> of the first complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> edition of Luther's works and later promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> edition. In 1530 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a> with his father, which won him the dislike of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>John Frederick succeeded his father as elector in 1532, ruling at first with his half-brother John Ernest and from 1542 alone. He set up an electoral consistory in 1542 and took a firmer stand than his father in favour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>. He opposed a council called by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, and asked Luther to summarise the articles he would uphold, which became the Schmalkald Articles. In 1541 he installed Nicolaus von Amsdorf as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a> in place of the elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-von-pflug">Julius von Pflug</a>, and in 1542 he expelled Duke Henry V of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> to protect Goslar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> of 1546 his cousin Duke Maurice invaded his lands. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a> on 24 April 1547 his army was dispersed, and a wound left a scar down his cheek. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> took him prisoner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The emperor condemned him to death as a rebel but did not carry out the sentence. To save his life and protect his family, John Frederick accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-of-wittenberg">Capitulation of Wittenberg</a>, giving up the electoral titles and the Duchy of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> to Maurice, and his sentence became life imprisonment. Maurice's attack on the emperor ended his captivity, and he was released on 1 September 1552. He returned to Wolfersdorf Castle, which he renamed Schloss Froehliche Wiederkunft, and moved his government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>. He planned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> in place of Wittenberg, and died in Weimar on 3 March 1554.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Frederick I of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ernestine elector <b>John Frederick I</b> led the Protestant Schmalkaldic League, and his defeat and capture by Emperor Charles V at the Battle of Mühlberg in 1547 cost his line the electoral title.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Torgau, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.56,13.01&amp;year=1536" aria-label="Torgau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John Frederick I, Elector of Saxony"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5686 5652h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5686 5652h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.56,13.01&amp;year=1536">What was here in 1536? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
